WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.130 --> 00:00:04.080
This beetle is going into the city to see
his lover.

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:06.600
She’s a dancer.

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:16.379
But this 1912 film is not just a staggeringly
weird tale of insect infidelity.

00:00:16.379 --> 00:00:23.439
It’s the true kickoff to a stop motion tradition
that has given us a ton of wildly different

00:00:23.439 --> 00:00:24.439
movies.

00:00:24.439 --> 00:00:26.179
But this invention didn’t come from Hollywood.

00:00:26.179 --> 00:00:40.270
It was made by an obsessive insect collector
in Lithuania who wanted to see insects dance.

00:00:40.270 --> 00:00:46.730
Stop motion is this combination of simplicity
and very, very tedious work.

00:00:46.730 --> 00:00:48.730
“Ah f..”

00:00:48.730 --> 00:00:55.329
An animator arranges objects in poses and
takes a picture.

00:00:55.329 --> 00:00:58.500
You move the object slightly and take another
picture.

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:02.760
Played successively, it looks like motion.”

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:07.610
You can tweak the process in a lot of ways
- adding more frames - and more precise movements,

00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:09.990
will make for a smoother animation.

00:01:09.990 --> 00:01:15.329
The potential of this illusion of movement
was obvious really quickly, like in 1908’s

00:01:15.329 --> 00:01:21.039
The Sculptor’s Nightmare, where busts briefly
moved or A Dream of Toyland, likely from the

00:01:21.039 --> 00:01:23.990
same year, which made toys come alive.

00:01:23.990 --> 00:01:33.310
But it took a European collector to elevate
it to an artform that changed the movies.

00:01:33.310 --> 00:01:38.670
Wladyslaw Starewicz was born in Moscow and
bounced around the pre-revolution Russian

00:01:38.670 --> 00:01:44.829
Empire, ending up in Kaunas - a city in modern
day Lithuania, then called Kovno.

00:01:44.829 --> 00:01:50.310
Some sources say Starewicz was a Natural History
museum director there (others say he just

00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:52.280
had a huge insect collection).

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:55.960
Either way, he had a problem.

00:01:55.960 --> 00:02:00.459
As he revealed later, he was commissioned
to make educational films “to show the life

00:02:00.459 --> 00:02:01.979
of the stag beetles.”

00:02:01.979 --> 00:02:06.700
He “waited days and days to shoot a battle
between two beetles, but they would not fight

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:08.420
with the lights shining on them.”

00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:12.950
So he started experimenting with making stationary
insects look like they were moving.

00:02:12.950 --> 00:02:17.910
He started with that stag beetle, which he
called by its scientific name: Lucanus Cervus.

00:02:17.910 --> 00:02:22.690
The goal was to show its fighting behavior,
but his next insect movie leapt to fiction

00:02:22.690 --> 00:02:24.610
to tell the tale of Helen of Troy.

00:02:24.610 --> 00:02:30.250
In 1912, The Cameraman’s Revenge — that
insect infidelity movie — became his most

00:02:30.250 --> 00:02:34.360
influential early work.

00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:37.910
See how this artist is actually painting another
beetle?

00:02:37.910 --> 00:02:43.230
Or how this grasshopper, filming Mr. Beetle’s
affair with a dragonfly, look how his tripod

00:02:43.230 --> 00:02:45.110
has individual legs.

00:02:45.110 --> 00:02:47.060
These miniscule touches were everywhere.

00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:52.810
He said he did it by installing wheels and
strings in each insect, and occasionally replacing

00:02:52.810 --> 00:02:55.780
their legs with plastic or metal ones.

00:02:55.780 --> 00:02:58.090
He used black threads to help move them.

00:02:58.090 --> 00:03:06.540
And it worked.

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:10.010
After the Russian Revolution, Starewicz fled
to Paris.

00:03:10.010 --> 00:03:11.570
He continued making films.

00:03:11.570 --> 00:03:17.210
By the time he made Frogland, he’d changed
his name from Wladislaw to Ladislas to make

00:03:17.210 --> 00:03:20.480
it easier to pronounce in French.

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:25.260
He continued to make incredibly influential
art — with stop motion — because “actors

00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:26.840
always want to have their own way.”

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:31.970
He had a host of popular films and stop motion
quickly influenced popular art and special

00:03:31.970 --> 00:03:32.970
effects.

00:03:32.970 --> 00:03:37.450
Starevich’s stop motion inspired the work
that was done in King Kong.

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:42.160
Terry Gilliam — the director and animator
behind the surreal Monty Python stop-motion

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:48.120
animations — said Starevich’s The Mascot
was one of the best animated films of all

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:49.120
time.

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:54.780
And Starevich’s masterpiece, Le Roman de
Renard clearly inspired Wes Anderson’s “The

00:03:54.780 --> 00:03:56.370
Fantastic Mr. Fox.”

00:03:56.370 --> 00:04:01.970
This combination of wild invention and obsessive
detail created a new art form.

00:04:01.970 --> 00:04:05.600
At the end of The Cameraman’s Revenge, the
grasshopper screens the movie he filmed

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:09.230
through a keyhole, the one of Mr. Beetle cheating
on his wife.

00:04:09.230 --> 00:04:12.010
She hits him with an umbrella.

00:04:12.010 --> 00:04:14.950
The movies changed forever.

00:04:14.950 --> 00:04:19.220
The beetles spent the night in jail.

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:25.230
That’s it for this one in this series about
big changes to movies that came from outside

00:04:25.230 --> 00:04:26.230
Hollywood.

00:04:26.230 --> 00:04:30.700
Stop motion’s a really global form, so I
want to know some of your favorite examples

00:04:30.700 --> 00:04:31.700
in the comments.

00:04:31.700 --> 00:04:36.560
I also want to leave you with a testimony
to Starevich’s work, which is that in some

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:41.870
of the early reviews, people were very very
impressed with how well he had “trained”

00:04:41.870 --> 00:04:48.070
his beetles to move around, and I honestly
don’t know if they were joking.

